At an iterate \((x_k,\lambda_k,\mu_k)\), the canonical SQP quadratic model is \[Q_k(s)=f(x_k)+
abla f(x_k)^Ts+	frac12s^TB_ks\] on the affine local feasible set \[\mathcal F_k=\{s:c(x_k)+J_c(x_k)s=0,\ g(x_k)+J_g(x_k)s\leq0\}.\] The SQP subproblem is \(\min_{s\in\mathcal F_k}Q_k(s)\). In exact-Hessian SQP, \[B_k=
abla_{xx}^2L(x_k,\lambda_k,\mu_k)=
abla^2f(x_k)+\sum_j\lambda_{k,j}
abla^2c_j(x_k)+\sum_i\mu_{k,i}
abla^2g_i(x_k).\] In approximate SQP, \(B_k\) is a specified symmetric surrogate for this Lagrangian curvature. The constant \(f(x_k)\) is optional because it does not affect the minimizers or KKT equations.

For minimizing \(f(x)\) subject to \(c(x)=0\), define \(L(x,\lambda)=f(x)+\lambda^{\mathsf T}c(x)\). At an iterate \((x_k,\lambda_k)\), the exact-Hessian SQP subproblem is \[\min_{d\in\mathbb R^n}\; 
abla f(x_k)^{\mathsf T}d+	frac12 d^{\mathsf T}
abla_{xx}^2L(x_k,\lambda_k)d \quad	ext{subject to}\quad c(x_k)+J_c(x_k)d=0.\] Thus the quadratic curvature matrix is exactly \(B_k=
abla_{xx}^2L(x_k,\lambda_k)\), rather than a quasi-Newton approximation.
